db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573837 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573837 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573837 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186573524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573837 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b308e7df-567f-5cd4-b865-053b904c951e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573854 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573854 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573854 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186573577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573854 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e6d75a63-b626-59fc-9980-1c756d4b224f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573874 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573874 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573874 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573874 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/63/6337a487598d8b5e432301b1a0d9a64407323a6bdcfa0f2fa7ff2ee140fac1bd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(5).$\n\n${f}^{′}(5)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17f4d138-65e0-5f89-8826-9793c7584c9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573874 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [4,6].$ Notice that $x=5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/42/42b4ddb29283047bd7ea8141ee14c793607b6b79a18f5d599a93839aec5a77b5.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (4,6)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{5-(-1)}{6-4}=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(5)=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573879 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/39431059174ad545642732d57f40689e13af962ed2bc091b86eb0d4f41717299.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=-2f(x)+3g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2177aae9-0cdc-5d1b-af35-640b6cd8ac62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573879 #edb/ref 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=-2{f}^{′}(x)+3{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33082034fa45d3228168431e8263c5a9f2c557ce26a8d637596a1e4e6d47d314.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-1}{3-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-1)}{3-1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573883 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573883 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573883 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573883 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f5f9c20bfeb54e816c98e75469f873d242b26143bdab65e56706861d8cbd7384.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573883 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44024ffd-d823-5830-bedd-a0c95dfca83c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573883 #edb/ref 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=3$ and $g(-2)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573898 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/34/34d404457497fea78bd8b4e03d9ab9f4cc270487f1c478cb52780748e52163d5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b5f58b1-1ad2-5dc4-9868-fc9b1d7a5b07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573898 #edb/ref 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c27964999163f87224059d85e5e6fe07a2e8b343e9fef1c34723bf9fbaaec1.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-(-2)}{2-(-1)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-1}{2-(-1)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & ={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =1+(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573920 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573920 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a8345270e511644cfb019099b907ecadff056f7efbd396e84d4313f3c3ab5817.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-2g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c089daaf-37cc-52fb-a2a0-6074ff898dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573920 #edb/ref 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (0,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09e4836acd54b814c1691c60003614d206fc2eee5cda6d7ad8d6222dd72d2d4f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{4-2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (0,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-0}{4-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573924 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c4fd9b46fdb02c23e04c50e437076d7a74f4823546b6608dbe537defb8a439fc.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c31fc413-2779-5fad-acf9-ef1563b305fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573924 #edb/ref 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=1$ and $g(-2)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1)}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
